-- Q7 [Mendelian Foundations] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Mendelian Foundations', 'A geneticist surveys a large natural population of beetles in which solid-black body is by far the most common coloration and several rarer colorations also appear. After labeling the solid-black form as the wild type, the geneticist is asked to justify that designation. Which feature of the solid-black form most directly accounts for its wild-type label?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "It is encoded by a dominant allele"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It arose by the most recent mutation"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It is the most common form in the population"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It is encoded by a recessive allele"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is C because the wild type is defined as the most common, standard phenotype or genotype in the population, against which variants are compared. The textbook states that the convention is to denote the most common phenotype or genotype among wild animals as the wild type, which is considered the standard or norm, while all other phenotypes or genotypes are considered variants that deviate from the wild type (OpenStax Biology 2e, lines 14937-14940). The wild-type allele is not defined by its dominance relationship at all: the textbook explicitly notes that a variant may be either recessive or dominant to the wild-type allele, so frequency alone determines the label. (Choice A) is incorrect because dominance is a separate property from the wild-type designation; although the wild-type allele is often dominant, the text states a variant may be dominant to the wild-type allele, so the label does not guarantee dominance. (Choice B) is incorrect because the wild type is the established standard form, not the newest one; variants are the deviations, and a new mutation would produce a variant rather than the wild type. (Choice C) is correct: the wild type is simply the most common, standard phenotype or genotype in the population, and the rarer colorations are its variants regardless of their dominance behavior. (Choice D) is incorrect because, like Choice A, it conflates frequency with a dominance relationship; the wild-type allele can be dominant or recessive relative to a given variant, and its label comes from being the standard, not from being recessive. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the definition of the wild type as the most common standard form in a population.', 'easy', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
