-- Q8 [Mendelian Foundations] medium skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Mendelian Foundations', 'A woman whose sibling has cystic fibrosis, an autosomal recessive disorder, has no symptoms of the disease but is found to carry one copy of the recessive allele. Which of the following statements about this woman is/are accurate?

I. She expresses a phenotype intermediate between affected and unaffected individuals.
II. She is homozygous recessive at the cystic fibrosis locus.
III. She can transmit the recessive allele to her children.', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is B because the woman is a heterozygous carrier (\(Aa\)) who is phenotypically unaffected yet still carries one recessive allele that she can pass to a gamete and thus to her children. A heterozygous individual who carries a recessive allele but does not express it is referred to as a carrier; the recessive allele is \"carried\" and not expressed because, in the presence of the dominant allele, the recessive allele is hidden and makes no contribution to the phenotype. Because each gamete receives only one of the two alleles, a carrier has a 50% chance of passing the recessive allele to any given child, even though she herself shows the dominant (unaffected) phenotype. Only statement III is therefore accurate. (Choice A) I only is wrong because statement I is false: a simple recessive carrier does NOT show an intermediate phenotype; an intermediate (blended) phenotype is the signature of incomplete dominance, a separate inheritance pattern in which the heterozygote displays a phenotype between the two homozygotes. (Choice B) is correct: statement III alone is accurate because a phenotypically unaffected heterozygote still harbors the recessive allele and, by the law of segregation, can transmit it to offspring. (Choice C) I and III only is wrong because it incorrectly accepts statement I; the carrier looks identical to a homozygous dominant individual, not intermediate. (Choice D) II and III only is wrong because it incorrectly accepts statement II; a carrier is heterozygous (\(Aa\)), not homozygous recessive (\(aa\))—a homozygous recessive individual would actually be affected by the disease. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that a heterozygous carrier shows the dominant phenotype while still transmitting the recessive allele.', 'medium', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 1, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
