-- Q11 [Dominance Patterns] medium skill4 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Dominance Patterns', 'A researcher types red blood cells for the M and N antigens in the offspring of one mating, summarized below.

| Parents (RBC antigens) | Offspring antigen phenotype |
|---|---|
| Mother: M and N; Father: M and N | 1/4 M only; 1/2 M and N; 1/4 N only |

The heterozygous offspring display both the M antigen and the N antigen on the surface of the same red blood cells. The inheritance of the M and N antigens is most consistent with which pattern?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Incomplete dominance"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Codominance"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Complete dominance"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Multiple alleles"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is B because the heterozygote simultaneously and fully displays both the M antigen and the N antigen, which is the defining signature of codominance. In codominance, both alleles for the same characteristic are expressed equally in the heterozygote, and the MN blood groups are the textbook example: homozygotes \(L^M L^M\) and \(L^N L^N\) display only the M or only the N antigen, while heterozygotes \(L^M L^N\) display both antigens, producing three phenotypically distinct genotypes. The cross between two heterozygotes nonetheless yields the standard Mendelian monohybrid \(1{:}2{:}1\) genotypic ratio, consistent with the observed \(1/4\) M : \(1/2\) MN : \(1/4\) N offspring. (Choice A) Incomplete dominance is incorrect because it would produce a single intermediate phenotype in the heterozygote (as in the pink \(C^R C^W\) snapdragon), not the distinct, simultaneous presence of both antigens. (Choice B) is correct: both alleles are fully and distinctly expressed in the heterozygote, the hallmark of codominance in the MN system. (Choice C) Complete dominance is incorrect because it would cause one allele to conceal the other, so the heterozygote would match one homozygote rather than express both antigens. (Choice D) Multiple alleles is incorrect because it refers to more than two allelic variants existing for a gene in a population (as with rabbit coat color), which is a separate concept from how two alleles interact in a single heterozygote. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret the antigen phenotypes and offspring ratios in the cross table and infer the underlying dominance mechanism.', 'medium', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 4, 105)
  RETURNING id
