oth appearing on red blood cells; the heterozygote would display both parental colors together (patches of crimson and white), not a single intermediate pink. (Choice C) Multiple alleles is incorrect because that pattern refers to more than two allelic versions of a gene existing in a population, whereas this cross involves only two alleles (crimson and white) and the three phenotypes arise from their dominance relationship, not from allelic diversity. (Choice D) is correct: the intermediate heterozygote phenotype and the \(1{:}2{:}1\) three-phenotype ratio together identify incomplete dominance. This is a Data-based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must interpret a phenotype tally and the heterozygote''s intermediate appearance to infer the underlying dominance mechanism.', 'hard', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 4, 150)
