-- Q14 [Dominance Patterns] medium skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Dominance Patterns', 'A woman with genotype \(I^A I^B\) has type AB blood, in which both the A and B antigens are detected on her red blood cells. Her brother, who is \(I^A i\), has type A blood. The contrast between these two genotypes most directly illustrates which inheritance pattern at the ABO locus?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Codominance of \\(I^A\\) and \\(I^B\\), with \\(I^A\\) dominant to \\(i\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Incomplete dominance of \\(I^A\\) and \\(I^B\\), with \\(I^A\\) dominant to \\(i\\)"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Codominance of \\(I^A\\) and \\(i\\), with \\(I^A\\) dominant to \\(I^B\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Simple dominance of \\(I^A\\) over both \\(I^B\\) and \\(i\\)"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is A because the \(I^A I^B\) heterozygote expresses both the A and B antigens simultaneously (codominance), while the \(I^A i\) individual is type A because \(I^A\) is fully dominant to \(i\). Codominance is the pattern "in which both alleles for the same characteristic are simultaneously expressed in the heterozygote," so an \(I^A I^B\) person shows both antigens and is type AB rather than an intermediate phenotype. The textbook states that for ABO, "the \(I^A\) allele encodes the A blood group antigen, \(I^B\) encodes B, and \(i\) encodes O," that "Both A and B are dominant to O," and that in \(I^A I^B\) offspring "both antigens are expressed equally," making ABO an example of multiple alleles and codominance. The \(I^A i\) genotype yielding type A shows that \(I^A\) masks \(i\) in classic dominant/recessive fashion, so the two relationships at this single locus differ. (Choice A) is correct: \(I^A\) and \(I^B\) are codominant because both antigens appear together in \(I^A I^B\), and \(I^A\) is simply dominant to the \(i\) (O) allele, exactly matching both observed phenotypes. (Choice B) names incomplete dominance, but incomplete dominance produces "an intermediate phenotype" such as a pink snapdragon from red and white parents; type AB blood shows both full antigens, not a blend, so the \(I^A\)/\(I^B\) relationship is codominance, not incomplete dominance. (Choice C) reverses the relationships: it labels \(I^A\) and \(i\) as codominant (which would make \(I^A i\) express an O-related antigen rather than being plain type A) and treats \(I^A\) as dominant to \(I^B\) (which would erase the B antigen and prevent a type AB phenotype), contradicting the observed AB and A blood types. (Choice D) calls \(I^A\) simply dominant to both \(I^B\) and \(i\); while \(I^A\) is dominant to \(i\), simple dominance over \(I^B\) would mask the B antigen and yield type A, not the observed type AB, so it cannot account for the codominant expression of both antigens. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall how codominance and simple dominance are defined and map each ABO genotype to its antigen phenotype.', 'medium', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 1, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
