-- Q15 [Dominance Patterns] medium skill1 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Dominance Patterns', 'A family pedigree shows that a dominant allele for a hereditary disorder is transmitted through three generations, yet several relatives who are confirmed to carry the allele never develop any symptoms of the disorder. Which phenomenon most directly accounts for these unaffected carriers?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Incomplete dominance of the disease allele"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Incomplete penetrance of the disease allele"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Codominance between the two alleles"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Environmental control of the trait"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is B because incomplete penetrance describes the situation in which only a fraction of the individuals who carry a disease-causing genotype actually express the corresponding phenotype, so some allele carriers remain unaffected. The OpenStax text establishes that an allele can be present in the genotype yet make no contribution to the observed phenotype: "in the presence of the dominant allele, the recessive allele is hidden and makes no contribution to the phenotype... recessive alleles can be ''carried'' and not expressed by individuals" (lines 14892-14894). Penetrance extends this carrier-versus-expression distinction to a dominant allele: when carriers of the allele can fail to show the trait at all, penetrance is incomplete, which is exactly the pattern described in the pedigree. (Choice A) Incomplete dominance is the case in which the heterozygote shows an intermediate phenotype, as in the pink snapdragon from a red-by-white cross (lines 14906-14908); it produces a blended phenotype in every heterozygote, not unaffected carriers, so it cannot explain individuals who show no phenotype at all. (Choice B) is correct: only a portion of genotypic carriers express the phenotype, leaving confirmed allele carriers unaffected, which is the definition of incomplete penetrance. (Choice C) Codominance is the case in which both alleles are simultaneously and fully expressed in the heterozygote, as with the M and N blood-group antigens (lines 14924-14928); this guarantees expression of both alleles rather than allowing carriers to escape expression. (Choice D) Environmental control refers to phenotypes such as temperature-dependent sex determination that arise from environmental rather than genetic differences and therefore are not inherited (lines 23111-23115); here the disorder is inherited as a transmitted allele, and the question asks why genetic carriers can be unaffected, which is penetrance rather than a purely environmental trait. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the distinction between carrying a disease genotype and expressing its phenotype and match it to the term incomplete penetrance.', 'medium', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 1, 105)
  RETURNING id
)
