The appearance of the rose phenotype in half the offspring most strongly indicates which pattern of inheritance?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Complete dominance of crimson over ivory"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Incomplete dominance between the two petal alleles"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Codominance of the crimson and ivory alleles"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Multiple alleles segregating at the petal locus"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is B because the heterozygous offspring display a single blended phenotype (rose) that lies between the two parental phenotypes, which defines incomplete dominance. In the backcross \(C^R C^I \times C^I C^I\), half the offspring are heterozygous \(C^R C^I\) and half are homozygous \(C^I C^I\); the heterozygotes show rose rather than crimson, demonstrating that neither allele is fully dominant. OpenStax states that incomplete dominance denotes \"the expression of two contrasting alleles such that the individual displays an intermediate phenotype,\" exactly as the snapdragon heterozygote \(C^R C^W\) produces pink flowers between red and white. The intermediate (rose) appearance of the heterozygote, not a parental or doubled phenotype, is the signature being tested here. (Choice A) Complete dominance is incorrect because the heterozygote would then resemble the crimson parent, not show an intermediate rose color; OpenStax describes dominant traits as fully expressed, so no blending would appear. (Choice B) is correct: the heterozygote''s intermediate phenotype is the defining feature of incomplete dominance, in which the heterozygote phenotype is intermediate between the two homozygous phenotypes. (Choice C) Codominance is incorrect because codominance requires that \"both alleles for the same characteristic are simultaneously expressed in the heterozygote\"—the heterozygote would show distinct crimson and ivory regions together, not a single blended rose, as in the MN blood groups where both M and N antigens appear. (Choice D) Multiple alleles is incorrect because that concept describes more than two alleles existing for a gene at the population level, which the data on a single two-allele cross neither shows nor requires. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of dominance patterns to a cross outcome and infer the underlying mechanism from the offspring phenotypes.', 'hard', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 150)
