-- Q17 [Mendel's Laws & Epigenetics] easy skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Mendel''s Laws & Epigenetics', 'A breeder self-crosses a heterozygous \(Rr\) pea plant, where \(R\) (round seed) is dominant to \(r\) (wrinkled seed). According to Mendel''s law of segregation, which statement most accurately describes the allele content of a single gamete this plant produces for the seed-shape gene?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "It carries exactly one allele, either \\(R\\) or \\(r\\)"}, {"label": "B", "text": "It carries both the \\(R\\) and \\(r\\) alleles together"}, {"label": "C", "text": "It carries the \\(R\\) allele more often than \\(r\\)"}, {"label": "D", "text": "It carries neither allele until fertilization occurs"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is A because Mendel''s law of segregation dictates that the two alleles of a gene separate during gamete formation so that each gamete receives exactly one of them. The textbook states the law requires that ''paired unit factors (genes) must segregate equally into gametes such that offspring have an equal likelihood of inheriting either factor,'' and the chapter summary specifies that ''alleles segregate into gametes such that each gamete is equally likely to receive either one of the two alleles present in a diploid individual.'' For a heterozygous \(Rr\) plant, this means every gamete carries a single seed-shape allele, and that allele is \(R\) in half the gametes and \(r\) in the other half. This equal, one-allele-per-gamete separation is the inheritance principle that produces the observed 3:1 phenotypic ratio in a monohybrid cross. (Choice A) is correct: each gamete receives exactly one of the two alleles, here either \(R\) or \(r\), reflecting the equal segregation of the paired factors. (Choice B) is wrong because it describes a failure of segregation; the law of segregation specifically requires the paired factors to separate so that a gamete never carries both alleles of the same gene. (Choice C) is wrong because segregation is equal, not biased toward the dominant allele; dominance governs which phenotype is expressed in an offspring, not how often each allele is packaged into a gamete, so \(R\) and \(r\) are passed to gametes with equal likelihood. (Choice D) is wrong because the allele is present in the gamete before fertilization; segregation places one allele into each gamete during gamete formation, and fertilization merely recombines two such gametes. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that the law of segregation places exactly one of a gene''s two alleles into each gamete.', 'easy', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 1, 70)
  RETURNING id
)
