-- Q18 [Mendel's Laws & Epigenetics] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Mendel''s Laws & Epigenetics', 'A geneticist self-crosses pea plants heterozygous for two seed genes, \(R\) (round, dominant to wrinkled \(r\)) and \(Y\) (yellow, dominant to green \(y\)), and scores a large \(F_2\) generation:

| \(F_2\) phenotype | Count |
|---|---|
| round, yellow | 315 |
| round, green | 108 |
| wrinkled, yellow | 101 |
| wrinkled, green | 32 |

These counts reduce to a \(9{:}3{:}3{:}1\) ratio. This outcome most directly demonstrates which principle governing the two genes?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "The two genes are linked on one chromosome"}, {"label": "B", "text": "The two genes interact through epistasis"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Each gene segregates one allele per gamete"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The two genes assort into gametes independently"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is D because a clean \(9{:}3{:}3{:}1\) dihybrid \(F_2\) ratio is the diagnostic signature that the two genes sorted into gametes independently of one another. Mendel''s law of independent assortment states that genes do not influence each other with regard to the sorting of alleles into gametes, so that a gamete receiving an \(r\) allele is equally likely to receive a \(Y\) or a \(y\) allele; this produces four equally likely gametes (\(YR\), \(Yr\), \(yR\), \(yr\)) and a 16-box Punnett square whose phenotypes fall in the \(9{:}3{:}3{:}1\) pattern. The recovery of all four phenotypic classes in exactly these proportions confirms that the texture and color genes assorted as independent events. (Choice A) Linked genes are located physically close on the same chromosome and tend to be transmitted together, so their alleles do NOT assort freely; the textbook notes that linkage means "the classical Mendelian prediction of a 9:3:3:1 outcome of a dihybrid cross would not apply," so the observed ratio rules linkage out rather than demonstrating it. (Choice B) Epistasis is a two-gene interaction in which one gene masks the other, and although such crosses still total 16 parts, they yield modified ratios such as \(9{:}7\), \(12{:}3{:}1\), or \(15{:}1\) — not the unmodified \(9{:}3{:}3{:}1\) seen here. (Choice C) The law of segregation correctly describes that each gene contributes one allele per gamete and underlies each individual 3:1 monohybrid split, but segregation alone does not specify how the two genes combine; the dihybrid \(9{:}3{:}3{:}1\) pattern specifically requires the second law, independent assortment, layering the two 3:1 ratios together. (Choice D) is correct: the \(9{:}3{:}3{:}1\) ratio collapses into two independent 3:1 ratios (3 round:1 wrinkled and 3 yellow:1 green), and applying the product rule \((3/4)\times(3/4)=9/16\) reproduces the dihybrid proportions, which is the hallmark of independent assortment. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the law of independent assortment to reason from an observed dihybrid phenotypic ratio back to the underlying inheritance principle.', 'hard', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
