-- Q19 [Mendel's Laws & Epigenetics] easy skill1 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Mendel''s Laws & Epigenetics', 'A gene that is silenced during the formation of gametes in one parent is transmitted to the offspring in its silenced state, yet sequencing reveals that the gene''s nucleotide sequence is identical in parent and offspring. Which mechanism most directly accounts for the heritable silencing of this gene?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A point mutation in the gene''s coding sequence"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Genetic recombination during crossing over"}, {"label": "C", "text": "DNA methylation of the gene"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Independent assortment of the parental alleles"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is C because DNA methylation is an epigenetic modification that silences a gene and can be transmitted to offspring without altering the gene''s nucleotide sequence. Epigenetic changes are inheritable changes in gene expression that do not result from changes in the DNA sequence; in DNA methylation, a methyl group is added to the cytosine of CG pairs in promoter regions, and methylated genes are usually silenced. The textbook notes that genes silenced during the development of the gametes of one parent can be transmitted in their silenced condition to the offspring (these are said to be imprinted), explaining heritable silencing with no change to the base sequence. (Choice A) A point mutation is a variation in the nucleotide sequence of the genome, so it would change the gene''s sequence between parent and offspring, contradicting the observation that the sequence is identical. (Choice B) Genetic recombination during crossing over physically exchanges DNA between homologous chromosomes to produce new allele combinations; it rearranges sequences rather than silencing a gene whose sequence is unchanged. (Choice C) is correct: DNA methylation adds a methyl group to the DNA, silencing the gene while leaving the underlying base sequence intact, and the methylation pattern can persist across a generational line. (Choice D) Independent assortment, Mendel''s second law, governs how alleles of different genes are distributed into gametes; it determines allele combinations but does not turn a gene off without changing its sequence. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that epigenetic silencing via DNA methylation is heritable and does not alter the DNA base sequence.', 'easy', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 1, 75)
  RETURNING id
)
