-- Q20 [Mendel's Laws & Epigenetics] medium skill1 ans=A
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Mendel''s Laws & Epigenetics', 'A researcher profiles the chromatin at a tumor-suppressor gene that is robustly transcribed in healthy cells. Which of the following chromatin features are most likely present at this gene''s promoter in the healthy cells?

I. Acetyl groups on the N-terminal tails of the surrounding histones
II. Heavy methylation of cytosines in the promoter''s CpG island
III. Loosely associated, widely spaced nucleosomes that expose the DNA', '[{"label": "A", "text": "I and III only"}, {"label": "B", "text": "I only"}, {"label": "C", "text": "III only"}, {"label": "D", "text": "II and III only"}]'::jsonb, 'A', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is A because an actively transcribed gene carries acetylated histones and loosely packed, widely spaced nucleosomes that expose the promoter, whereas CpG-island methylation is associated with silencing rather than active transcription. Adding acetyl groups to the histone tails makes the histone charge less positive, so the binding of the negatively charged DNA to the histones is relaxed and the chromatin opens to transcription (statement I). This loosening alters nucleosome spacing: when nucleosomes are spaced far apart the DNA is exposed and transcription factors can bind, allowing gene expression (statement III). By contrast, DNA methylation occurs within CpG islands in promoter regions, and methylated genes are usually silenced, so heavy CpG methylation is the hallmark of an inactive, not an active, promoter (statement II is excluded). (Choice A) is correct: histone acetylation and the resulting open, widely spaced nucleosome configuration are exactly the modifications that accompany a transcribed gene, while methylation is left out because it silences. (Choice B) is wrong because it omits statement III; the acetylation in statement I and the loosened, widely spaced nucleosomes in statement III describe the same open state and both apply to an active promoter. (Choice C) is wrong because it omits statement I; histone acetylation is a primary cause of the open chromatin and must accompany the exposed-DNA configuration at an active gene. (Choice D) is wrong because it includes statement II; CpG-island methylation usually silences a gene, so it would not be present at a robustly transcribed promoter, even though the open nucleosome state in statement III is correct. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that histone acetylation and open nucleosome spacing activate transcription whereas CpG methylation silences genes.', 'medium', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 1, 110)
  RETURNING id
)
