-- Q21 [Mendel's Laws & Epigenetics] medium skill2 ans=C
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Mendel''s Laws & Epigenetics', 'A female cat heterozygous for an X-linked coat color gene has a tortoiseshell coat in which the two colors appear as discrete contiguous patches rather than as a uniform intermediate blend. The patchy arrangement is most directly explained by the fact that', '[{"label": "A", "text": "each coat color allele is incompletely dominant to the other"}, {"label": "B", "text": "the coat color alleles undergo independent assortment during gamete formation"}, {"label": "C", "text": "all cells descended from one embryonic cell inherit the same inactive X"}, {"label": "D", "text": "both X chromosomes are inactivated together early in development"}]'::jsonb, 'C', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is C because X inactivation is random in each early embryonic cell but is then clonally inherited, so a contiguous patch of skin descends from a single progenitor cell whose descendants all share the same silenced X and therefore express the same coat color allele. In female mammalian embryos, one X chromosome in each cell inactivates by condensing into a Barr body; "the chance that an X chromosome (maternally or paternally derived) inactivates in each cell is random, but once this occurs, all cells derived from that one will have the same inactive X chromosome or Barr body" (lines 16306-16308). Because a heterozygote will "express one of two different coat colors over different regions of their body, corresponding to whichever X chromosome inactivates in that region''s embryonic cell progenitor" (lines 16310-16312), each region is a clone, producing discrete patches rather than a blended phenotype. (Choice A) Incomplete dominance would yield a single uniform intermediate phenotype across the whole animal, which is the opposite of the discrete-patch pattern; the passage attributes the variegation to X inactivation, not to an allelic dominance interaction (lines 16310-16312). (Choice B) Independent assortment describes how alleles of different genes distribute into gametes and operates before fertilization, so it cannot explain a spatial mosaic arising within the body of a single individual during embryonic development (lines 16310-16312). (Choice C) is correct: random silencing fixed by clonal descent means every cell in a contiguous region carries the same inactive X and expresses the same allele, generating sharp-edged color patches rather than a uniform blend. (Choice D) Only one X is inactivated per cell, not both; an individual "will inactivate all but one X chromosome in each of her cells," so at least one X always stays active to provide essential gene products (lines 16321-16322). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principles of random X inactivation and clonal inheritance to explain an observed spatial coat-color pattern.', 'medium', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 110)
  RETURNING id
)
