-- Q22 [Mendel's Laws & Epigenetics] hard skill2 ans=D
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Mendel''s Laws & Epigenetics', 'A gene exists in a single functional form with no sequence differences among individuals in a population. When this gene is inherited from the father, it is expressed in the offspring; when the identical allele is inherited from the mother, it is silent. Which finding most directly accounts for why expression of this gene tracks the parent of origin rather than the inherited DNA sequence?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "A point mutation in the maternal coding sequence abolishes the protein"}, {"label": "B", "text": "One X chromosome is randomly inactivated in each somatic cell"}, {"label": "C", "text": "The two parents contribute unequal numbers of allele copies"}, {"label": "D", "text": "The maternal copy is methylated during her gametogenesis and inherited silenced"}]'::jsonb, 'D', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is D because an imprinted gene is methylated and silenced during one parent''s gametogenesis and is transmitted to offspring in that silenced state, so its expression depends on which parent supplied it rather than on the DNA sequence. DNA methylation occurs at CpG islands in promoter regions, and methylated genes are usually silenced; the textbook states that "genes that are silenced during the development of the gametes of one parent are transmitted in their silenced condition to the offspring" and "such genes are said to be imprinted" (lines 19533-19535). Because the methyl tags "do not alter the DNA base sequence" (line 19523), the maternally and paternally inherited copies are sequence-identical, yet only the unmethylated paternal copy is transcribed, producing parent-of-origin-dependent expression. (Choice A) is incorrect because a point mutation would change the maternal DNA sequence, contradicting the premise that the gene has "no sequence differences"; imprinting silences a sequence-identical allele through methylation, not mutation (lines 19523, 19533). (Choice B) is incorrect because random X-inactivation silences one X chromosome at random in each somatic cell regardless of parental origin, so it cannot produce consistent expression tied to which parent transmitted the allele. (Choice C) is incorrect because each parent contributes one allele copy per gene under Mendel''s law of segregation, which states that paired factors "segregate equally into gametes" (line 15221); imprinting differs in the chemical state of those equal copies, not their number. (Choice D) is correct: the parental gamete-stage methylation is carried into the offspring unchanged, making expression a function of parent of origin rather than sequence, and the imprint can be erased and reset in the next generation''s gametes. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of methylation-based imprinting to explain a parent-of-origin expression pattern in a specific cross.', 'hard', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 160)
  RETURNING id
)
