-- Q23 [Mendel's Laws & Epigenetics] hard skill2 ans=B
WITH q AS (
  INSERT INTO questions (section, topic, subtopic, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, difficulty, foundation, content_category, discipline, cognitive_skill, estimated_time_seconds)
  VALUES ('bio_biochem', 'Genetics and Evolution', 'Mendel''s Laws & Epigenetics', 'A pea plant heterozygous for seed color and seed shape, with the two genes on separate chromosomes, produces four kinds of gametes—\(YR\), \(Yr\), \(yR\), and \(yr\)—in equal proportions, so that the non-parental allele combinations \(Yr\) and \(yR\) appear just as often as the parental combinations. This equal representation of every allele combination across the two genes is most directly attributable to which principle?', '[{"label": "A", "text": "Equal segregation of paired alleles"}, {"label": "B", "text": "Independent assortment of the two genes"}, {"label": "C", "text": "Dominance of one allele over another"}, {"label": "D", "text": "Linkage of genes on a chromosome"}]'::jsonb, 'B', 'This is a Biology question that falls under the content category ''Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.'' The answer is B because the equal appearance of every combination of alleles across two different genes—including the non-parental \(Yr\) and \(yR\) gametes—is the defining signature of independent assortment. Mendel''s law of independent assortment states that genes do not influence each other with regard to the sorting of alleles into gametes, and every possible combination of alleles for every gene is equally likely to occur. Because the color gene and the shape gene sort without influencing each other, a gamete that received the \(y\) allele is equally likely to carry \(R\) or \(r\), yielding all four gamete classes \(YR\), \(Yr\), \(yR\), and \(yr\) in equal proportion. The question asks which principle governs combinations across different genes, and that is precisely what independent assortment describes. (Choice A) Equal segregation explains why a single heterozygous gene contributes one allele of that gene to each gamete with equal likelihood, but by itself it does not predict how alleles of two different genes combine; segregation alone would still leave open whether the genes mix freely or stay together, so it cannot account for the equal frequency of the non-parental combinations. (Choice B) is correct: independent assortment specifies that every combination of alleles across the two genes is equally likely, which is exactly the equal four-way gamete ratio described. (Choice C) Dominance governs which of two alleles for the same characteristic is expressed in the phenotype of a heterozygote; it says nothing about the frequencies with which allele combinations are packaged into gametes. (Choice D) Linkage would produce the opposite outcome—genes located close together on the same chromosome tend to be transmitted together, so the parental combinations would predominate and the non-parental \(Yr\) and \(yR\) gametes would be underrepresented rather than equally frequent. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply Mendel''s laws to a described gamete distribution and identify which principle, rather than the meiotic mechanism, accounts for the equal mixing of allele combinations across two genes.', 'hard', '1C', 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation', 'biology', 2, 150)
  RETURNING id
)
